#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""
Stream Flattrade order updates over a websocket and merge them into the Redis hash `flattrade:orders:orders`, and keep
streaming until stopped.

    bin/flattrade/orders/websocket_order_details
    redis-cli HGETALL flattrade:orders:orders
    redis-cli HGET flattrade:orders:orders <norenordno>

Self-contained: it logs in with `FlattradeAPI` and carries its own connection to Flattrade's Noren stream at
`wss://piconnect.flattrade.in/PiConnectWSTp/`, so it needs no other feed process or key.

**The stream.** This is a connection of its own, carrying no market data. After it opens, the client sends the
connect frame - `{"t": "a", "uid": ..., "actid": ..., "source": "API", "accesstoken": ...}` - and once Noren
acknowledges it with `{"t": "ak", "s": "OK"}`, subscribes to the account's orders with `{"t": "o", "actid":
...}`. From then on every change to an order arrives as an `om` frame of abbreviated string fields:
`norenordno`, `exchordid`, `status`, `rejreason`, `exch`, `tsym`, `token`, `trantype`, `prd`, `prctyp`, `ret`,
`qty`, `fillshares`, `cancelqty`, `dscqty`, `prc`, `trgprc`, `avgprc`, `norentm`, `exch_tm`, `remarks` and more.
Noren keeps the session alive with its own `{"t": "h"}` heartbeat every three seconds.

**The key.** `flattrade:orders:orders` is the one hash for Flattrade's orders, keyed by `norenordno` and shared with
`bin/flattrade/orders/api_order_details`, which merges the polled order book into it. Each update is written as
`{"observed_at": <epoch received>, "source": "websocket", "order": <normalized order>, "data": <om frame>}`, with
Noren's frame exactly as sent in `data`. An `om` frame carries the order book's fields, so `order` comes from the same
mapping the poller uses - see `bin/flattrade/orders/api_order_details` for its fields. A websocket update always replaces its order's
entry; a polled row replaces it only when the entry was observed before the poll's request was sent. The hash expires
at 06:00 IST, moved to the next 06:00 by every write.

**The stream.** Every update is also appended, in arrival order, to the Redis Stream
`flattrade:order-updates:stream` as an `update` field holding the same JSON, which `bin/flattrade/orders/store_orders_to_db`
writes to `flattrade.order_updates` - so the history the hash overwrites is kept there. Capped at about 20,000
entries.

**Positions.** Noren's order stream carries orders only, so this script writes no positions; `bin/flattrade/portfolio/positions`
fills `flattrade:portfolio:positions` from the REST position book.

**Sessions.** When Noren refuses the connect frame, or the socket fails to open six times running, it logs in
again through `FlattradeAPI`, confirms the new session against `UserDetails` - Noren refuses a dead session
inside an HTTP 200 - and only then reconnects. The token is read afresh on every connect, so a login made by
any process is picked up. A reconnect otherwise backs off from 1 to 60 seconds. Updates arrive only when an
order changes, so a quiet socket is not a broken one.

Exits 0 when stopped with Ctrl+C or SIGTERM, and 1 when a login fails or the socket is still refused after
logging in again. Reads only - it never places, modifies or cancels an order.
"""

import sys
import json
import time
import signal
import threading
from datetime import datetime, timedelta
from pathlib import Path
